WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:04.920
Let's be real: Even if you've managed to get
Oscar winners to don tights and trade quips

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:08.660
with space raccoons, you're bound to drop
the ball every once in a while.

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:14.160
Try not to cringe too hard as we dive headfirst
into the MCU's worst casting decisions ever.

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:19.060
Thor: The Dark World is one of the most forgotten
entries of the MCU.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:23.940
Sure, it set up another Infinity Stone, gave
Jane Foster and the gang something to do,

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:28.740
and brought Loki back after, of course, seemingly
killing him off earlier in the same flick.

00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:29.740
But that's about it.

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:31.180
It's just kind of there.

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:36.980
A huge part of Dark World's failure to resonate
was Eccleston's limp portrayal of Malekith.

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:42.020
Malekith, a dark elf currently at the center
of Marvel's "War of the Realms" comic event,

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:47.079
is a malevolent trickster with a taste for
wicked turnabouts and gleeful cruelty.

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:51.360
Eccleston's embodiment reflects exactly, uhh...
none of this.

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:53.620
"Darkness returns, Asgardian.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:57.680
Have you come to witness the end of your universe?"

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:04.320
His aims are vague something something return
the universe to darkness, yada yada the rightful

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:10.300
destiny of the dark elves: standard villain
gobbledegook and his personality is no better

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:10.800
defined.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:16.440
If you remember anything, it's his round-eyed
mask and elaborately braided hair and having

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:20.480
your performance overshadowed by the costuming
department is never what an actor should aim

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:20.980
for.

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:25.260
It's hard to waste the smooth, icy Mads Mikkelsen
as a villain.

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:30.560
From Casino Royale to Hannibal, he's spent
most of his career sending chills down audiences'

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:31.060
spines.

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:35.220
And when the story around him is well-told
and acted, that makes it a doubly impressive

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:37.020
feat to somehow bungle his character.

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:42.380
Solid writers, story, colleagues, and costuming,
plus the dude who played Le Chiffre as the

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:42.880
villain?

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:44.760
How could that possibly go wrong?

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:47.040
Doctor Strange showed us how.

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:51.680
Despite the fact that it's a dizzyingly shot,
snappily-written origin story and despite

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:56.680
how brilliant Benedict Wong and Benedict Cumberbatch
are in their principal roles Mikkelsen's Kaecilius,

00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:00.820
the magic-user who serves the fiendish Dormammu,
is utterly forgettable.

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:05.580
Like Malekith, the most memorable thing about
his character is the make-up wizardry performed

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:09.560
upon him to make it look as though his skin
is flaking away beneath his eyes.

00:02:09.649 --> 00:02:13.959
Beyond that, uhhh… there are a few cool
tricks he does with his magic blades?

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:16.319
Maybe a quip or two that landed okay?

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:21.380
He is totally wasted in the role and entirely
overshadowed by the rest of the largely enjoyable

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:21.880
movie.

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:24.480
At least he's got the rest of his filmography
to fall back on.

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:28.840
Mickey Rourke is definitely not forgettable
as Ivan Vanko.

00:02:28.849 --> 00:02:32.640
He gobbles up scenery with a spoon, letting
the light catch in his gold teeth whenever

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:36.860
he can, brandishing his Russian accent like
a saber at every scene partner.

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:37.720
"Hey, man.

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:41.820
Don't get too attached to things.

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:44.220
Learn to let go."

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:49.040
Many entries on this list are here because
they are cinematic non-entities, entirely

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:52.760
swallowed up in the shadows of other actors,
special effects, and the ever-growing tower

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:54.360
of MCU worldbuilding.

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:55.520
That ain't the case here.

00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:59.760
Iron Man 2 is widely considered one of the
lesser Marvel movies, but if you remember

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:02.700
anything about it, you remember Ivan Vanko.

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:05.020
But honestly, is that really a good thing?

00:03:05.329 --> 00:03:10.019
Rourke is a bit too present in the role as
an actor, he never disappears into the character.

00:03:10.019 --> 00:03:14.280
It's almost entertaining on its own to watch
Rourke put so much energy into Vanko, but

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:16.769
ultimately, it distracts from everything around
it.

00:03:16.769 --> 00:03:20.989
Years after the fact, you don't recall the
family debts the character was trying to repay

00:03:20.989 --> 00:03:24.540
you remember the accent, the teeth, the bird,
the whips.

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:28.020
You remember everything around the character
but not the character himself.

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:30.920
And in movie terms, that's an unforgivable
flaw.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:34.659
Quick: remember everything you can about Christine
Palmer.

00:03:34.659 --> 00:03:38.900
If that name doesn't ring a bell, and it probably
doesn't, just replace it with "Doctor Strange's

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:39.400
girlfriend."

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:43.340
Okay, you might reply, she was a doctor too,
and there's that one scene they have in a

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:46.450
closet together in the hospital, and you're
pretty sure you remember the actress from

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:47.580
that Notebook movie.

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:50.180
If you did any better than that, you should
be commended.

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:54.000
Christine Palmer is, effectively, a girlfriend-shaped
prop.

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:58.449
This isn't exactly unique there are a lot
of forgettable Girlfriend X roles that talented

00:03:58.449 --> 00:03:59.840
actresses make the best of.

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:05.360
But some of them see Hayley Atwell's Peggy
Carter or Zoe Saldana's Gamora manage to break

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:06.860
out and become something more.

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:09.640
McAdams' Christine Palmer just kind of…
stands around.

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:14.139
Like Kaecilius, the general psychedelia of
the movie surrounding her serves to overshadow

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:18.660
her completely, and really, that isn't entirely
a bad thing it's a pretty incredible spectacle,

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:19.160
after all.

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:23.780
Still, we get the feeling that even the Time
Stone wouldn't be able to bring McAdams' unmemorable

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:25.340
performance back from the dead.

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:28.000
"This … Some things just can't be fixed."

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:33.040
If there was ever a moment Iron Fist seemed
like a good idea, no one remembers it.

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:37.140
Now that it's been officially canceled, the
cultural autopsy can be performed, and by

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:40.980
far the biggest problem is Finn Jones' weak
performance.

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:47.720
Danny Rand is let's face it reality-shatteringly
annoying, and he never quite stops being so.

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:52.260
He's a young billionaire searching for himself,
and yet somehow, he's even more irritating

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:53.540
than that description implies.

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:57.950
His quest for identity makes him seem too
often like a brat at worst, a boring "also-ran"

00:04:57.950 --> 00:05:00.750
of a Hero's Journey protagonist at best.

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:04.290
When you're not rolling your eyes at him,
you're yawning and, when it comes to TV, a

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:06.690
yawn is arguably worse than a cringe.

00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:12.910
When someone mentions Quicksilver, your mind
probably jumps to Evan Peters' zippy, sarcastic

00:05:12.910 --> 00:05:14.660
portrayal in the X-Men movies.

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:19.560
Watching him turn moments of terror into high-speed
slapstick performances is an absolute delight,

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:21.740
something between a ballet and a Marx Brothers
spoof.

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:26.971
He's a bright spot in an increasingly dark
franchise that no one anticipated, a total

00:05:26.980 --> 00:05:28.000
joy and surprise.

00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:28.900
Oh.

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:29.940
Then there's this dude.

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:36.180
"You didn't see that coming?"

00:05:38.060 --> 00:05:40.280
You might remember him from Avengers: Age
of Ultron.

00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:42.600
On the other hand, you might not remember
him at all.

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:45.140
It's not actor Aaron Taylor-Johnson's fault,
really.

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:49.400
He couldn't have known he'd end up compared
to Evan Peters' unexpectedly brilliant take

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:49.960
on the character.

00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:55.600
But the fact is that his portrayal of Pietro
Maximoff is, well, unremarkable.

00:05:55.740 --> 00:05:59.000
He has the right moves, of course, and his
tragic death is, well, tragic.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:03.150
But there's a reason Wanda, his sister who
has remained a part of the MCU, never seems

00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:04.760
to bring him up: no one cares.

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:07.880
Taylor-Johnson did his job and nothing more.

00:06:08.140 --> 00:06:10.300
He ran, he quipped, he died.

00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:15.380
In a movie that contained James Spader, Scarlett
Johansson, and Robert Downey Jr., you can't

00:06:15.390 --> 00:06:18.330
exactly rake the dude over the coals for falling
by the wayside.

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:23.580
Natalie Portman is a talented, versatile,
and beloved actress that much we can all agree

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:24.080
on.

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:29.220
That said… her MCU character, Jane Foster,
is one of the MCU's greatest failures.

00:06:29.230 --> 00:06:33.450
Sure, she gives it the old college try in
the first Thor movie, injecting Foster with

00:06:33.450 --> 00:06:37.420
a charm, intelligence, and excitement at the
prospect of wormholes and other dimensions.

00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:43.400
But even then, her heart was clearly not in
it, and by the time Thor: The Dark World rolled

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:45.840
around, it was hard to deny that she had checked
out.

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:49.800
Jane just doesn't seem very connected to the
world around her, let alone the developments

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:53.940
happening within it, and the performance fades,
especially in comparison to any of her other

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:54.440
roles.

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:58.460
You can't win 'em all, but if you're Natalie
Portman, you can take comfort in your Oscar

00:06:58.460 --> 00:07:01.640
and the fact that you're basically a superhero
yourself.

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:06.590
Believe it or not, but there was a time when
people laughed at the idea of a Guardians

00:07:06.590 --> 00:07:07.600
of the Galaxy movie.

00:07:07.770 --> 00:07:11.500
We're glad that time has past but it's still
crazy to imagine that viewers have gotten

00:07:11.510 --> 00:07:17.680
behind a talking raccoon, a sentient tree,
and a morally malleable, blue-skinned space…

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:18.460
nanny.

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:21.320
"I'm Mary Poppins, y'all!"

00:07:21.460 --> 00:07:22.780
R.I.P., brother…

00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:23.580
R.I.P.

00:07:23.780 --> 00:07:27.710
Anyway, Guardians of the Galaxy went on to
become a huge hit and a pillar of the modern

00:07:27.710 --> 00:07:28.200
MCU.

00:07:28.340 --> 00:07:32.920
But as much as we love Star-Lord and his merry
band of misfits, we don't really care much

00:07:32.930 --> 00:07:35.750
for the movie's bad guy, Ronan the Accuser.

00:07:35.750 --> 00:07:39.990
It's no surprise why: he's exceptionally boring
in a movie that is anything but.

00:07:39.990 --> 00:07:44.580
He's mad about space warfare that the audience
has no real stake in, carries a giant hammer,

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:47.320
wears dramatic eyeliner, and… that's pretty
much it.

00:07:47.730 --> 00:07:51.980
When he showed up again in Captain Marvel,
Marvel made sure he was toting his hammer

00:07:51.980 --> 00:07:54.520
in every shot, lest we forget who he was.

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:56.550
And who could blame us for doing so?

00:07:56.550 --> 00:08:00.620
If we remember Ronan for anything, it's for
being beaten in a galactic dance-off.

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:05.680
Look: We're not saying Ronan's yawn-inducing
characterization is actor Lee Pace's fault.

00:08:06.020 --> 00:08:11.440
In fact, if Ronan's whole shtick is being
single-mindedly lawful evil, and super grouchy,

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:13.400
then Pace has got this guy down pat.

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:18.150
What we are saying is that pretty much anybody
in the pantheon of Marvel baddies would've

00:08:18.150 --> 00:08:20.620
made a more interesting villain than this
dude.

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:26.720
Emil Blonsky is an elite soldier who becomes
Abomination, the endpoint of the darkest path

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:28.040
Bruce Banner could take.

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:32.920
Nothing stops him from gobbling up power he
has no kindly girlfriend, noble attitude,

00:08:32.920 --> 00:08:33.940
or passion for science.

00:08:34.100 --> 00:08:36.500
He just wants to be bigger, better, and burlier.

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:41.680
His fight against the Hulk is a spectacular
one in which, as Banner goes on to sheepishly

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:44.360
recall in The Avengers, they, quote, "break
Harlem."

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:45.220
Cool, right?

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:51.100
Banner versus a grim reflection of himself,
power against the looming specter of corruption,

00:08:51.100 --> 00:08:53.560
two giant man-beasts fighting to the death.

00:08:53.740 --> 00:08:59.400
And yet, despite the memorable climax, Tim
Roth as Blonsky simply fails to deliver.

00:08:59.410 --> 00:09:03.850
One wonders if, had the stars aligned to make
this early effort more like the MCU we know

00:09:03.850 --> 00:09:08.160
now, all that potential could have been actualized
the way the Hulk deserves.

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:08.900
But it wasn't.

00:09:09.480 --> 00:09:13.440
Roth never gets to the core of his conflict
with Banner, nor the nature of his own evil.

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:16.200
He's just a bad guy, doing bad things for
the sake of it.

00:09:16.300 --> 00:09:19.680
This gleeful, one-note evil can still lead
to great cinema Willem Dafoe as the crazed

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:22.240
Green Goblin comes to mind but that is very
much not the case in The Incredible Hulk.

00:09:22.540 --> 00:09:25.880
Roth does what he has to do, and absolutely
nothing more.

00:09:27.140 --> 00:09:32.680
Inhumans was a total flop for which which
Serinda Swan can only be partially blamed.

00:09:32.920 --> 00:09:34.120
Nothing about it worked.

00:09:34.580 --> 00:09:38.340
The special effects were cartoonish when they
weren't outright bizarre, the translation

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:43.491
of the Inhumans' fantastical world from page
to screen never rose above awkward, and even

00:09:43.491 --> 00:09:48.170
the best and most experienced members of the
cast seemed ill-suited to their characters.

00:09:48.170 --> 00:09:53.470
It came and went without much of a fuss few
were excited for it, few saw it, and few discuss

00:09:53.470 --> 00:09:55.020
it now that's done and dusted.

00:09:55.020 --> 00:09:57.500
It was, to put it simply, a failure.

00:09:57.740 --> 00:09:59.860
So no, it's not entirely Swan's fault.

00:10:00.230 --> 00:10:04.920
But if there is one person who embodies the
slow-motion implosion that was Inhumans, it

00:10:04.940 --> 00:10:05.440
is her.

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:10.140
Part of it is her costuming her wig is atrocious
on every level, from its oddly stiff motion

00:10:10.140 --> 00:10:11.900
to its Ronald McDonald shade of red.

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:14.660
Some of it is the absurd situations the writers
placed her in.

00:10:14.780 --> 00:10:18.320
But most of it is the maudlin melodrama she
brings to her scenes.

00:10:18.540 --> 00:10:24.200
There is no moment of Inhumans that more perfectly
symbolizes the project's sheer stupidity than

00:10:24.200 --> 00:10:30.290
watching Swan deliver a drawn-out, feral-cat-in-the-alleyway
yowl as her hair is shorn by the fiendish

00:10:30.290 --> 00:10:31.280
Maximus.

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:34.920
It's meant to be a moment of cruelty, but
it's straight-up hilarious to watch her crumble

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:38.940
at the touch of a decidedly unfantastic pair
of electric clippers.

00:10:39.080 --> 00:10:43.220
Nothing can stop the Inhumans, apparently,
except a hasty haircut.

